{
  "$comment": "Cutover tooling for #553 Phase 1 (issue #582) — the converge-verify check between the phone's legacy PowerSync-side local store and the legacy mirrored Postgres tables. Removed by #556 along with the two implementations it pins. See spec/converge_verify/README.md; these vectors are FROZEN and hand-authored — never regenerate one to make a test pass.",
  "spec_version": 1,
  "column_kinds": [
    "verbatim_text",
    "integer",
    "boolean_as_int",
    "timestamp_utc_ms"
  ],
  "excluded_columns": {
    "*": [
      "user_id"
    ],
    "todos": [
      "time_spent_minutes"
    ]
  },
  "canonical_encoding": {
    "shape": "A JSON array of the manifest's columns, in manifest order, encoded with the explicit rules below rather than a stdlib JSON writer — the two languages' writers disagree on control-character escaping, and a digest may not depend on which side computed it.",
    "rules": [
      "No whitespace anywhere between tokens: elements are joined with ','.",
      "SQL NULL emits the literal null.",
      "An integer emits its shortest decimal form, '-' prefixed when negative.",
      "A string emits a double-quoted JSON string.",
      "Inside a string, escape exactly: '\"' as \\\", '\\' as \\\\, U+0008 as \\b, U+0009 as \\t, U+000A as \\n, U+000C as \\f, U+000D as \\r, and any other code point below U+0020 as \\u00 followed by two LOWERCASE hex digits. Every other code point is emitted literally as UTF-8 — no \\u escaping of non-ASCII, and U+007F is not escaped.",
      "A value the manifest's kind refuses emits a single-key object sentinel and records a per-row anomaly: {\"unparseable_timestamp\":\"<raw as text>\"}, {\"invalid_boolean\":\"<raw as text>\"}, {\"invalid_integer\":\"<raw as text>\"}, {\"invalid_text\":\"<raw as text>\"}, or {\"missing_column\":true}. A refusal is never an exception: the report must survive whatever the store holds.",
      "row_digest is the lowercase hex SHA-256 of the canonical string's UTF-8 bytes."
    ]
  },
  "raw_as_text": [
    "Every sentinel and every anomaly record renders the refused value through one shared rule, so the two implementations cannot disagree about how a bad value is spelled.",
    "A string renders as itself.",
    "A boolean renders as 'true' or 'false' — never Python's 'True'.",
    "An integer, and a floating-point value with no fractional part, render as a decimal integer.",
    "SQL NULL renders as 'null'.",
    "Anything else renders as '<number>' for a remaining numeric value and '<unknown>' otherwise. The exact value is deliberately dropped rather than formatted: shortest-round-trip float formatting is not guaranteed identical across the two languages, and a digest may not depend on which side computed it. The anomaly still names the table, row id and column, which is what a reviewer needs to go and look."
  ],
  "column_kind_rules": {
    "verbatim_text": [
      "A string emits verbatim — including the empty string, which is distinct from NULL.",
      "Anything else is refused as invalid_text."
    ],
    "integer": [
      "An integer emits as a JSON number.",
      "A floating-point value with no fractional part emits as the corresponding integer (SQLite may hold a REAL in an INTEGER column).",
      "A boolean, a numeric string, or a fractional value is refused as invalid_integer."
    ],
    "boolean_as_int": [
      "0 and false emit 0; 1 and true emit 1.",
      "Any other value is refused as invalid_boolean — a truthiness rule would hide the divergence it is there to show."
    ],
    "timestamp_utc_ms": [
      "A timestamp-aware value (a Postgres timestamptz row) converts to UTC.",
      "A naive timestamp value is assumed to be UTC.",
      "A string is parsed by the tolerant rules in timestamp_parsing below.",
      "The instant truncates (never rounds) to millisecond precision and emits as YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.mmmZ with a four-digit year.",
      "Anything the rules refuse is refused as unparseable_timestamp."
    ]
  },
  "timestamp_parsing": {
    "grammar": "^[ \\t]*(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})[Tt ](\\d{2}):(\\d{2})(?::(\\d{2})(?:\\.(\\d+))?)?[ \\t]*(Z|z|[+-]\\d{2}(?::?\\d{2})?)?[ \\t]*$",
    "rules": [
      "Surrounding spaces and tabs are trimmed. The class is spelled out as [ \\t] rather than \\s on purpose: the two languages' regex engines disagree about which exotic code points \\s covers, and a grammar the two sides read differently is the one thing this file exists to prevent.",
      "The date/time separator is 'T', 't', or a single space.",
      "Seconds are optional; a fractional part is any number of digits and is truncated to its first three (never rounded).",
      "The offset is optional and may be preceded by whitespace — Drift's store_date_time_values_as_text output writes a space before it (docs/SYNC.md). 'Z'/'z' means UTC; otherwise ±HH:MM, ±HHMM or ±HH.",
      "No offset means UTC. This is an assumption, not an observation: the app writes UTC (time_logs, focus_sessions) and Drift writes an offset, so a zone-less string is a legacy or foreign shape.",
      "Component ranges are validated explicitly: month 1-12, day valid for that month and year, hour 0-23, minute 0-59, second 0-59 (no leap second), offset hour 0-23, offset minute 0-59.",
      "The resulting UTC year must be 1-9999 — the intersection of what the two languages' date types can both represent and format.",
      "These are RULES, not one observed serialisation. The compose stack and the deployed stack run different powersync-service versions, so freezing on a captured wire format would pin the wrong thing."
    ],
